add('Which text-image safety findings survive careful measurement?',
    'VLM safety research: experiments, corrections, negative results, and the next decision',
    data=[('Aryaman Singh Dev','Advisor discussion | Prof. Thao Minh Le\nPrepared 21 October 2026'),('Current position','Historical evidence + completed development studies\n64-unit window human review still pending')],
    notes='Opening: We began with a proposed safety trade-off between text and vision. That simple prediction did not hold in our exploratory tests. We then asked a more practical question: when the same model behaves differently for text and an image, what part comes from the image, the instruction, and the measurement? This talk traces the experiments and the corrections, including failures. The purpose is to agree on a defensible contribution and next evidence step, not to announce a finished novel result.',source='docs/STATUS_AND_PAPER_PLAN.md, sections 1-3 and 17-18',tag='ADVISOR PROGRESS REVIEW')
add('We have evidence; we do not yet have a validated novelty claim',
    'Three facts to keep in view throughout the discussion',data=[('Observed','Wording/content and image presence affect historical refusal gaps; effects depend on the checkpoint.'),('Not observed','The chosen matched crossover did not appear. Longer windows changed no automated labels.'),('Still open','Human interpretation of the new 64 units; fresh validation; a contribution beyond close prior work.')],
    notes='Say what succeeded without implying every hypothesis passed. We built reliable collection and review tooling, tested controls and recorded negative results. Publication novelty is a separate question from whether code ran successfully. The 64 units are not 64 independent generations: they are two windows of 32 generated answers.',source='Status sections 3, 10, 15-17; v7_window_core8/results_window.json')
add('The original idea evolved after testing', 'A failed starting prediction led to a more precise evaluation question',data=[('01 / Starting idea','A text-vision safety see-saw: improving refusal in one channel should worsen the other.'),('02 / Observation','Historical text/image refusal often moved together; tested steering raised both, including benign over-refusal.'),('03 / Current question','Which apparent carrier effects survive matched questions, fixed instructions, consistent cohorts and reviewed labels?')],
    notes='The original advisor framing was a trade-off. The saved correlations and steering experiment do not support that simple zero-sum account. This is limited evidence, not proof that no trade-off can exist. Positive correlation does not prove a shared internal mechanism. The research pivot is from a broad mechanistic story to a controlled measurement question.',source='v1/v2 saved coupling and v2 steering; status sections 1 and 3',take='Refusal moving together is an observation, not a causal mechanism.')
add('Why a text-versus-picture gap can mislead', 'One benchmark comparison can change several things at once',data=[('Content','Full question versus a shortened activity phrase'),('Instruction','Answer normally versus perform an activity in detailed steps'),('Carrier / attachment','Text versus rendered text; an image can matter even when unrelated'),('Measurement','Refusal, harmful assistance, misunderstanding, missing parses and token caps')],
    notes='Use a harmless example verbally, such as a troubleshooting question represented in plain chat or in a picture. If both the question form and instruction change, the difference cannot be uniquely attributed to vision. Our B/E contrast also changes content form, so the earlier shorthand mostly the wrapper was too strong. Full-question paired grids are the cleaner wording comparison.',source='Status sections 1, 3; P1-P4',take='Controls make a comparison more interpretable; no one control resolves every ambiguity.')
