add('A filtering demonstration exposed a denominator trap','Retrospective analysis of the original assisted human labels',kind='table',data=[['Analysis population','Plain image−text','Steps image−text','Apparent reversal?'],['All 8 items in each condition','+12.5 pp','+37.5 pp','No'],['Keep UNDERSTANDING separately','−50 pp','+50 pp','Yes, but different items'],['Same retained items in all 4 cells','0 pp','+100 pp','No; only 3 items']],
 notes='This was an explicitly naive sensitivity exercise, not a claim about the model suddenly changing. Generic refusals often leave comprehension uncertain. Excluding those answers separately by condition changes the populations being compared and can manufacture a reversal. Correct common-cohort matching removes the strict negative plain effect here. The tiny selected cohort is still not an estimate of latent comprehension. This known selection issue is a promising domain-specific audit example, but not established methodological novelty and not proof that MMJailBench makes this mistake.',source='reports/comprehension_selection_2026-10-20.json; analyze_comprehension_selection.py; P2/P9',take='UNCERTAIN comprehension is not evidence that the model failed to understand.')
